From: metaljax <oleg@eterevsky.com>
Date: Sat, 23 Aug 2026 09:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] eval: signal attached events when the walk exits by throwing

`eval_impl(async=true)` attaches a per-stream `Event` to every array it
visits and signals those events only in a tail loop AFTER the walk. Any
exception raised from a primitive's eval -- a Metal library that will not
build is the case we hit -- escapes before that tail runs, so every array
already visited keeps an attached event at a value nothing will ever
signal. `array::wait()` waits on it with `waitUntilSignaledValue(value,
-1)`, i.e. no timeout, so the next blocking `eval` of any of those arrays
hangs the process forever. A reportable error becomes an unkillable wedge.

Minimal repro (macOS, Metal), hangs at the last line before this change:

    import mlx.core as mx

    a = mx.array([1.0, 2.0, 3.0]) * 2.0          # lazy; the kernel's INPUT
    bad = mx.fast.metal_kernel(
        name="bad", input_names=["x"], output_names=["out"],
        source="  _this_identifier_does_not_exist;")
    out = bad(inputs=[a], grid=(1, 1, 1), threadgroup=(1, 1, 1),
              output_shapes=[(3,)], output_dtypes=[mx.float32])[0]

    try:
        mx.async_eval([a, out])                  # walks `a`, then throws
    except Exception as exc:
        print("threw as expected:", exc)

    mx.eval(a)                                   # <-- waits forever
    print("reached")

`a` is walked (and evaluated) before the kernel's build failure, so it is
left `status=evaluated` with an unsignaled event; the `eval(a)` that
follows takes the "nothing unscheduled" path in `eval` and blocks in
`array::wait()`.

The fix is symmetric with what the Metal backend already does for the
other half of this problem -- `CommandEncoder::commit`'s completion
handler signals the events of a command buffer that failed, with the
comment "Metal won't signal the events for us on error, manually signal
them to avoid infinite waiting". This is the same guarantee for a failure
raised on the HOST, mid-walk: a scope guard signals the events on an
exceptional exit and is dismissed once the tail loop is reached, so the
normal path is unchanged. It swallows secondary failures deliberately --
it runs while an exception is already propagating and the caller is owed
that one.

Only `async_eval` can create the poisoned arrays (the attachment is
`if (async)`-guarded), but the guard covers both paths for free.
--- a/mlx/transforms.cpp
+++ b/mlx/transforms.cpp
@@ -225,6 +225,40 @@
   }
 
   std::set<Stream> open_streams;
+
+  // The events attached below are signaled by the tail loop after the walk.
+  // If the walk exits by THROWING -- a Metal library that will not build, an
+  // allocation the device refuses -- that loop never runs, and every array
+  // the walk has already visited keeps an attached event at a value nothing
+  // will ever signal. `array::wait()` waits on it with no timeout
+  // (`waitUntilSignaledValue(value, -1)`), so the next `eval` of any of
+  // those arrays hangs the process: a reportable error becomes a wedge.
+  //
+  // This guard signals them on the way out and is dismissed once the tail
+  // loop is reached, so the normal path is exactly what it was. It swallows
+  // secondary failures deliberately: it runs while an exception is already
+  // propagating, and the caller is owed THAT one.
+  struct AbandonedEvents {
+    std::set<Stream>* streams;
+    std::unordered_map<uint32_t, Event>* events;
+    ~AbandonedEvents() {
+      if (streams == nullptr) {
+        return;
+      }
+      try {
+        for (auto& s : *streams) {
+          if (auto e = events->find(s.index); e != events->end()) {
+            e->second.signal(s);
+          }
+          if (s.device == Device::gpu) {
+            gpu::finalize(s);
+          }
+        }
+      } catch (...) {
+      }
+    }
+  } abandoned{&open_streams, &events};
+
   while (!tape.empty()) {
     auto arr = std::move(tape.back());
     tape.pop_back();
